   "note": "THE QUICK START'S LED TABLE IS THE MOST COMPLETE IN THIS FAMILY. System: \"PSU1/2 - Green (OK), Amber (fault)\", \"DIAG - Green (OK), Orange (fault detected)\", \"FAN - Green (OK), Orange (fault)\", \"LOC - Blinking Blue (OK)\", \"ALARM - Green (OK), Red (alarm)\". Ports: \"QSFP-DD Port LEDs: Blue (400G), Cyan (200G), Green (100G), Yellow (50G)\", \"QSFP28 Port LEDs: Green (100G), Cyan (50G), Magenta (40G), Blue (25G), Yellow (10G)\", \"SFP28 Port LEDs: Blue (25G), Green (10G), Cyan (1G)\". Management: \"Left (link), Right (activity)\". And the timing pair: \"BITS/ToD LEDs: Green (valid BITS), Blinking Green (1PPS ToD)\". So every lamp on this device carries a stated vocabulary, including the two that the BITS and ToD jacks share. THOSE TWO ARE PLACED RATHER THAN INTEGRATED, and that is a deliberate choice about which truth to draw. The elevation shows them in the upper corners of the BITS jack, which is where common/rj45-ganged-eth@1 would draw integrated lamps - but that part says ETHERNET, and docs/rj45-family-design.md reserves it for Ethernet jacks while a timing jack takes the bare std/rj45-ganged@2. Fitting the Ethernet part here to get two lamps would misdeclare the port to get the picture right. So the jacks are bare and the lamps are two placements at 385.70 and 396.55, which is where the corners are. It also says more than the integrated pair could: the quick start's \"BITS/ToD LEDs\" are ONE PAIR SERVING TWO JACKS - green for valid BITS on the upper, blinking green for 1PPS ToD on the lower - and separate placements can carry a `for:` each. An integrated pair belongs to one jack and could not."
